WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.069
Your own Claude Code setup, this week.

2
00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:04.675
Apply the routine to what you already run.

3
00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:05.979
Five steps.

4
00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:07.581
First, inventory.

5
00:00:07.861 --> 00:00:14.901
Run claude mcp list and note every server and its scope: local (only you, this project), project (from a.

6
00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.184
mcp.

7
00:00:16.464 --> 00:00:20.027
json file in the repository), or user (everywhere).

8
00:00:20.307 --> 00:00:21.779
Remove what you don’t use.

9
00:00:22.059 --> 00:00:26.944
The guide’s single most important recommendation is to know what you are actually running.

10
00:00:27.224 --> 00:00:29.571
Second, mind project-scoped servers.

11
00:00:29.851 --> 00:00:30.491
A.

12
00:00:30.771 --> 00:00:31.773
mcp.

13
00:00:32.053 --> 00:00:36.213
json file in a repository you clone can define servers.

14
00:00:36.493 --> 00:00:50.701
Claude Code asks you to approve them in interactive sessions, but its docs note that non-interactive runs ( claude -p ) and Agent SDK sessions cannot show that prompt and load project-scoped servers without asking.

15
00:00:50.981 --> 00:01:01.669
If you run Claude Code in CI or scripts on repositories you did not write, use --strict-mcp-config with an explicit --mcp-config, or exclude project settings.

16
00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:04.061
Third, set permissions by tool name.

17
00:01:04.341 --> 00:01:09.696
Each MCP tool has a full name such as mcp__harbor__update_issue_status.

18
00:01:09.976 --> 00:01:14.797
Allow the read tools you trust, keep write tools on ask, and deny what you never want.

19
00:01:15.077 --> 00:01:17.083
Fourth, isolate what deserves it.

20
00:01:17.363 --> 00:01:20.819
Claude Code’s sandbox does not cover MCP servers.

21
00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:26.944
A server that handles untrusted content and holds a powerful credential belongs in a container.

22
00:01:27.224 --> 00:01:29.123
Fifth, watch for the trifecta.

23
00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:35.824
For each session, ask whether the agent can read private data, read untrusted text, and send data out.

24
00:01:36.104 --> 00:01:37.747
If all three, remove one.

25
00:01:38.027 --> 00:01:41.184
Other hosts expose the same levers under different names.

26
00:01:41.464 --> 00:01:51.917
Gemini CLI’s per-server trust setting bypasses all confirmations for that server and defaults to off, and its include and exclude lists limit which tools load.

27
00:01:52.197 --> 00:02:03.867
OpenAI’s MCP documentation says it does not verify custom servers, recommends connecting only to servers you trust, and keeps approvals on for consequential actions.

28
00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:06.557
Whatever the host, the questions are the same.
